WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:04.060
Accents are strange things.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:08.790
A language can be spoken using the exact same
words but in hundreds of different ways.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:14.059
Sometimes accents are so dissimilar that two
speakers of the same language with different

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.640
accents can’t even understand one another.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.750
We’ve had accents for thousands of years.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.660
The Bible speaks of a tribe that identified
their enemies by the fact that they couldn’t

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.570
correctly pronounce the word “shibboleth”.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.260
So they basically killed everyone with a funny
accent.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.260
Nice.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.610
But it’s not just humans that have accents.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.500
Animals also tweet, bahh and moo differently
depending on where they come from.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.800
The UK is well known for its large and varied
array of regional accents.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.570
A study in the UK found that cows in England
moo differently in different parts of the

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:49.570
country.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.450
Their moo’s seemed to somewhat reflect the
accent of their owner.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.210
But what actually causes accents?

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.420
Let’s find out.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.850
Humans have a remarkable talent for being
able to quickly detect an accent.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:08.500
We’re actually really, really good at it.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:13.799
Humans can detect if someone has a different
accent from themself after just 30 milliseconds

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:15.079
of speech.

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:20.659
We can even do the same thing if we listen
to that 30ms of speech played backwards on

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:21.659
a recording.

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:24.469
Well done brain, you’re smarter than you
look.

00:01:24.469 --> 00:01:29.450
So where does this superhuman, accent-divining
power come from?

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.459
It all starts from when we’re only an itty-bitty
little baby.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:38.420
Humans aren’t just inherently good at learning
language; it’s a lot simpler than that.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.439
During our first few years we’re hyper-receptive
to noises around us.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:46.700
We quickly learn to imitate those noises.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.709
Babies subconsciously listen in to people’s
conversations around them and so that’s

00:01:51.709 --> 00:01:56.729
what the child learns to imitate, those people’s
languages and speech patterns.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:02.090
Just because a baby is born to Spanish parents
in Spain it doesn’t mean that Spanish is

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.759
destined to be its native tongue.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:09.970
If that newborn Spanish baby is placed in
say, England, from birth, adopted by English

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.190
parents, it will grow up speaking fluent English.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.379
Not only that, it will have an English accent,
not Spanish.

00:02:18.379 --> 00:02:23.230
In fact this noise imitation goes further
than we initially thought.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:28.430
During a study bird noises were played to
an infant child, soon afterwards the child

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.540
was able to accurately imitate those bird
noises.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:37.410
But children quickly lose this ability to
effortlessly reproduce noises, as they grow

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.410
up.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.620
That’s why it’s significantly harder for
an adult to learn a new language than a child.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.680
But that doesn’t mean you can’t change
your accent.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.530
It’s significantly harder to do so as you
grow up, but when an adult moves to a new

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.890
area, they do slowly lose their accent in
favour of a new one.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.110
Although this can take several years.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:02.019
A famous example of this is when Britain colonised
America.

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:07.790
Over several years the settlers lost their
traditional Queen’s English accent in favour

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:12.459
of, well the American accent, specifically
southern drawl.

00:03:12.459 --> 00:03:17.840
The English gave America words like “y’all”
but that’s more of a dialect issue than

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.840
an accent.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.930
It’s believed that accents are formed because
of variations in the physical makeup of different

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.709
ethnicities.

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:30.689
Different ethnic groups are born with different
shaped noses, mouths and throats; these subtle

00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:34.110
differences alter the way that words are formed.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:39.140
For example a group of people with slightly
wider than average throats, bigger noses and

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:42.330
larger chests will speak in a deeper pitch.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:47.750
On the other hand, people with a narrower
throat, smaller nose and smaller chest will

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:51.689
speak with a more nasally and higher pitched
accent.

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:55.010
This can been seen with individuals of East-Asian
origin.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:04:00.519
Due to their smaller than average body frames
they tend to sound higher-pitched when speaking

00:04:00.519 --> 00:04:03.150
English than Caucasians, who will say the
exact same words but typically in a deeper

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.150
voice.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.019
But that’s not the only reason we have accents.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:10.140
Physical variations such as this only have
subtle effects on our accent.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:14.609
The factors most responsible for different
accents are influences from different languages

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:16.799
and cultures.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:20.160
Specifically the mixing of those different
languages and cultures.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.570
Humans like to explore and it’s because
of our explorative nature that different regions

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.170
have different accents.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:30.380
For example, when the Germanic people came
to Britain in the 5th century they brought

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.350
their language and culture with them.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:39.070
Over time this changed the way people in England
spoke, creating new accents.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:44.470
Because people from all kinds of different
regions and cultures came to Britain, today

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:49.560
Britain has an estimated 56 different major
accents across the country.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.390
That’s a hell of a lot for the relatively
small island that it is.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.380
For that you can thank the huge wealth of
other countries the UK is parked next to.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:04.650
The location where the overseas settlers first
came ashore and made their home determined

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.690
what accent that region of Britain would ultimately
have.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.600
Britain’s a great example but this exact
same reasoning holds true for any country

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.400
that has different accents, which is most
countries.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.670
You may be wondering what’s the difference
between an accent and a dialect?

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:27.310
An accent is the way one sounds when speaking;
it’s the unique way in which people pronounce

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:32.240
words and the musicality, if you like, of
their voice.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.560
A dialect however, refers to the linguistic
system as a whole, particularly the grammatical

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.340
features of the way a person talks.

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:44.750
To put all that more simply, a dialect is
what people say and an accent is the way they

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.060
say it.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:47.060
As for me?

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.360
Well I have a west Lancashire accent, which
is why I my accent doesn’t sound like the

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.180
traditional plummy boarding school type accent,
which people from around the world usually

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.990
associate with England.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.580
That accent by the way, which some people
simply refer to as “Queen’s English”

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:06.100
is actually called “Received Pronunciation”.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:10.170
America has a similar, so called “general,
accent-less accent”.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:16.380
It’s called “General American” it’s
a mix of Midwest and west coast accents.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:21.870
Received Pronunciation and General American
are often regarded as the standard accent-less

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.930
accents of the English language, but this
is not true.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.570
There is no such thing as “not having an
accent”.

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:34.450
Even Received Pronunciation and General American
themselves are accents.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:39.890
So to summarise, we have accents because humans
like to explore and when two different cultures

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:45.610
come together in the same location the unique
ways in which they both talk get amalgamated

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:49.930
into a brand new accent.

